 ## Overview
-Gaussian Geometry Parameters Prediction is a method for initializing and refining the geometric attributes of 3D Gaussian primitives in a feed-forward manner, typically using a global volumetric representation rather than per-pixel local volumes [S2]. It solves the problem of redundant, inconsistent, and ghosting artifacts that arise when accumulating locally predicted Gaussians from multiple views into a global coordinate system [S2]. By predicting geometry in a unified 3D space and refining positions via recursive offsets, it ensures global consistency and reduces floating artifacts caused by noisy depth predictions [S2].
+Gaussian Geometry Parameters Prediction is a method for initializing and refining the geometric attributes of 3D Gaussian primitives in a feed-forward manner, typically using a global volumetric representation rather than per-pixel alignment [S2]. It solves the problem of redundant, inconsistent, and ghosting artifacts that arise when accumulating locally predicted Gaussians from multiple views [S2]. Unlike sibling methods that rely on per-scene optimization or local cost volumes, this approach predicts a unified global scene representation by refining noisy depth-initialized points in 3D space, thereby improving global consistency and reducing floating artifacts [S2].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Inputs:** A set of $N$ sparse input images $\{I_n\}_{n=1}^N$, their calibrated intrinsic matrices $\{K_n\}_{n=1}^N$, and extrinsic matrices $\{T_n = R_n | t_n\}_{n=1}^N$ [S2]. Off-the-shelf metric depth estimators provide depth maps $\{D_n\}_{n=1}^N$ for each frame [S2].
-*   **Outputs:** A set of 3D Gaussians $\mathcal{G} = \{(\mu_i, \alpha_i, \Sigma_i, c_i)\}_{i=1}^{N_p}$, where $\mu_i$ is the center, $\alpha_i$ is opacity, $\Sigma_i$ is the covariance matrix, and $c_i$ is the view-dependent color [S2].
