 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Feed-forward inference is significantly faster than per-scene optimization methods (like standard 3DGS), which require minutes of gradient descent. The method involves a single pass through a 3D CNN and MLP heads, plus recursive offset updates (typically 2 iterations for convergence) [S2].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing a global feature volume $F$ and the point cloud $P$. The sparse 3D CNN reduces memory usage compared to dense volumes, but it is still higher than pixel-aligned methods that do not maintain a global 3D structure [S2].
-*   **Performance:** Achieves real-time rendering speeds due to the feed-forward nature and efficient rasterization of 3D Gaussians [S2]. It improves global consistency and reduces ghosting artifacts compared to existing feed-forward methods that predict per-pixel aligned Gaussians using local cost volumes or epipolar transformers [S2].
-*   **Strengths:** Handles large-scale urban scenes with large camera movements and small parallax, where traditional multi-view stereo methods fail [S2]. Effectively removes floating artifacts and redundant primitives [S2].
-*   **Limitations:** Relies on the quality of off-the-shelf monocular depth estimators; noisy depth can lead to initial inaccuracies, though the recursive refinement helps [S2]. Requires calibrated camera poses for unprojection [S2].
-*   **When to Use:** Suitable for urban view synthesis, autonomous driving applications, and large-scale scene reconstruction where global consistency is critical and per-scene optimization is too slow [S2].
-*   **When Not to Use:** Not suitable for small-scale scenes with high overlap where local cost volumes might be more efficient, or when camera poses are unknown/unavailable [S2].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Feed-forward inference is significantly faster than per-scene optimization methods, which require minutes of gradient descent [S2]. The complexity is dominated by the sparse 3D CNN feature extraction and the MLP decoding per point [S2].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storage for the global point cloud $P$ and the feature volume $F$. The sparse 3D CNN reduces memory usage compared to dense volumes [S2].
+*   **Measured Performance:** EVolSplat achieves real-time rendering speeds for urban scenes [S2]. It improves global consistency and reduces ghosting artifacts compared to pixel-aligned methods like MVSplat, Flash3D, and DrivingForward [S2].
+*   **Strengths:** Handles large-scale urban scenes with sparse inputs; reduces redundancy and ghosting artifacts; enables real-time rendering via feed-forward prediction [S2].
+*   **Limitations:** Relies on the quality of monocular depth estimators; may lose high-frequency appearance details if primitives are downsampled too aggressively (addressed by IBR-based color prediction) [S2].
+*   **When to Use:** Urban view synthesis, autonomous driving applications, and scenarios requiring fast, feed-forward reconstruction from sparse views [S2].
+*   **When Not to Use:** Small-scale scenes with high overlap where per-scene optimization is feasible and higher fidelity is required; scenes with significant dynamic elements without temporal modeling [S2].
 
 ## Background
-Gaussian Geometry Parameters Prediction, specifically in the form of EVolSplat, was introduced by Sheng Miao, Jiaxin Huang, Dongfeng Bai, Xu Yan, Hongyu Zhou, Yue Wang, Bingbing Liu, Andreas Geiger, and Yiyi Liao from Zhejiang University, Huawei Noah’s Ark Lab, and the University of Tübingen [S2]. It was published in CVPR 2025 [S2]. The method addresses the limitations of existing feed-forward 3DGS methods, such as MVSplat, Flash3D, and DrivingForward, which suffer from multi-view inconsistencies and duplicated content when accumulating local predictions [S2]. It builds on the foundation of 3D Gaussian Splatting [15] introduced by Kerbl et al., which explicitly parameterizes the 3D radiance field as a collection of 3D Gaussians for efficient rendering [S2].
